over the past several years apple has  dominated the top of the fortune 500  from being the first company to reach a  1 trillion valuation to being the first  company to reach a 3 trillion valuation  much of this success is often credited  to steve jobs which makes sense given  that he was the visionary who gave apple  some of its most iconic ideas and  attributes with that being said though  tim cook has now been the ceo of apple  for over 10 years and during that time  period he's had some of the best  execution and the least drama of any  silicon valley executive from overseeing  apple's foray into services to leading  new product launches such as the apple  watch airpods the m1 chip and  potentially soon the apple car and apple  glasses there's no doubt that apple  wouldn't be what they are today without  tim cook but it seems like cook's  journey at the top is nearly coming to  an end rumors suggest that cook is  looking into a succession plan and that  he'll retire after he oversees the  launch of one more major product  category considering this i thought it  would be a great time to look back at  cook's past and analyze how he became  the ceo of apple  taking a look back tim's story dates  back to november 1st 1960 to a small  town in alabama called mobile he grew up  with two brothers named gerald and  michael in a pretty average american  household tim's father donald worked at  a shipyard while his mother joel dean  worked at a pharmacy it seems that his  parents were strong christians as tim  would be baptized as a kid  anyway tim would end up attending  robertsdale high school where he quickly  became a star student he graduated rank  2 in his class and he would enroll in a  local state college called auburn  university while tim always wanted to  work at a computer company it doesn't  seem like he wanted to be a programmer  or a tech person per se  it seemed like he was always more  interested in the operation side of  companies and appropriately he majored  in industrial engineering one of his  first jobs after graduation was at ibm  where he mainly worked on their supply  chain while working at ibm tim also  secured an mba from duke university in  1988 this allowed him to climb the  corporate ladder even quicker and tim  soon became a north american fulfillment  director at ibm  despite this rapid progress though tim  decided to leave ibm after 12 years at  the company in 1994 tim was hired as the  ceo of a small computer retailer named  intelligent electronics he worked here  for the next three years before jumping  over to compact computer corporation as  vice president  around the same time apple was doing  extremely bad  revenue was diving off a cliff and the  company was on the verge of bankruptcy  meanwhile steve jobs had been away from  the company for over a decade it wasn't  until apple agreed to acquire next  software that steve would return to  apple originally steve was just supposed  to be a temporary ceo but it quickly  became clear that no one else was better  for the role especially given the  circumstances as steve assembled a new  executive team to hopefully revive  apollo one of the people he invited to  join the company was of course tim cook  at first tim wasn't sure if he was  actually going to join apple but one  thing he was sure of was that he would  at least meet with steve jobs like many  of us tim himself was a massive fan of  steve who he thought was a creative  genius but at the meeting steve talked  to tim about his vision for what would  eventually become the imac and after  hearing steve describe his vision tim  knew he was gonna do it when tim shared  this news with his friends and family  they all thought that he was crazy to  give up an executive role at an  established computer company in order to  join one that was nearly extinct was  completely irrational tim admits that  there's no way to logically justify his  actions but with that being said it tim  also knew that working with steve was a  once in a lifetime opportunity so tim  listened to his gut and joined apple in  1998  despite joining a company that's known  for their beautiful products and ui tim  never really worked on either of these  aspects he knew that his expertise was  within the supply chain and that's where  he focused his efforts as senior vice  president of worldwide operations tim's  first order of business was purging  apple's existing supply chain to reduce  complexity tim slashed the number of  supply chain vendors from over a hundred  down to just 24. he also reduced the  number of warehouses by half and he  started shifting apple towards contract  manufacturing  contract manufacturing it is basically  when you hire third-party companies to  handle various parts of the production  and assembly process like tsmc and  foxconn these companies also work with  dozens of other tech companies which  allows them to reach extraordinary  economies of scale aside from clearing  up the supply chain tim spent a lot of  his effort on inventory management one  of the crown jewels of apple's supply  chain is just in time or jit  manufacturing and its roots can be  traced back to tim  jit manufacturing is the idea of  eliminating inventory in an ideal jit  implementation stores would carry  absolutely no inventory this isn't to  say that you can't go to the store and  buy the given product  if you were to go to the store the  product should ideally arrive at the  store seconds before you buy it  obviously this isn't very realistic but  tim was able to leverage jit to reduce  apple's inventory from weeks of  inventory to less than 5 days worth of  inventory  this might not sound like a big deal but  if you've ever run a business and  especially a small business you know  that buying and storing inventory is  extremely expensive take the iphone for  example apple stopped releasing unit  sales numbers for the iphone 8 years ago  but let's just assume that it's 100  million units per year  if apple were to store five weeks worth  of inventory they would have to sync  five billion dollars in inventory if  apollo only had to store five days worth  of inventory though they would only have  to sink 685 million dollars into  inventory  again this doesn't really matter that  much today as they have plenty of cash  but back in the early 2000s this was  crucial to cutting costs  and with the help of contract and  charity manufacturing which reduced  costs and the help of the imac and the  ipod which increased revenue apple was  finally able to regain profitability  this allowed steve to focus on the  iphone which was of course a massive  success but all was not good steve was  diagnosed with pancreatic cancer which  made it rather difficult for him to  juggle apple and his personal health at  one point steve actually needed a liver  transplant and some sources suggest that  tim offered to donate a portion of his  liver but steve declined the offer while  steve declined tim's offer as steve's  health continued to decline he delegated  more and more tasks to tim until tim was  eventually made interim ceo in 2009  steve would come back later that year  but in early 2011 he would take another  medical leave of absence before he  eventually resigned in august of 2011  which made tim cook the permanent ceo  the first few years of tim's leadership  were rather easy given that iphone  adoption was still yet to peak there  were also plenty of new obvious features  to introduce like touch id and a larger  screen but iphone sales would eventually  not only peak but start to decline and  this is when tim's skills as a ceo would  really be put to the test tim addressed  his decline with the two-fold solution  and the first part was making apple a  lifestyle company while apple products  have always been expensive it wasn't  till the last couple of years that  iphone started getting a reputation for  being overpriced now don't get me wrong  here people have criticized the price of  the iphone since the day that it was  launched but i think the price argument  has shifted substantially initially the  argument had less to do with the device  itself being overpriced and more to do  with it being too expensive for the  average person to buy  today the argument has shifted more  towards the device itself being  overpriced but ironically entry-level  iphones are actually cheaper than ever  before  apple also keeps around older  generations for longer than ever before  and the iphone se starts at 429 which is  cheaper than the original iphone  i think all of this is part of tim's  strategy to maintain an apple's  exclusive image if apple's presentations  focused on how their entry level phones  are cheaper than ever before that would  likely degrade apple's image however by  focusing on the pro and the pro max  versions that cost over a thousand  dollars tim is brilliantly able to get  the media to paint apple's products as  exclusive and desirable i believe other  overpriced products like the apple  wheels and the pro stands serve the same  purpose these products simply define  apple's image while apple's real revenue  drivers are their cheaper products aside  from crafting this genius balance  between brand image and practical  products tim also reduced apple's  dependence on the iphone by embracing  services icloud apple music and apple tv  have all been massive hits for apple as  of october 2020 apple literally had 585  million paid subscribers even if each of  these subscribers only pay apple five  dollars per month we're talking about  nearly three billion dollars in revenue  per month and 35 billion revenue per  year considering this it's not  surprising that apple's revenue figures  are higher than ever before even though  iphone sales peaked seven years ago  aside from these services providing  apple with a great source of revenue  these services also strengthen the apple  ecosystem and make it harder and harder  for users to leave and i think these  three factors are what will become tim's  legacy at apple he defined apple's brand  image he significantly reduced the  dependence on iphone sales and he  crafted the apple ecosystem  looking forward it it looks like tim is  excited to release one more product  which is likely either the apple glasses  or the apple car  the glasses would be a major step into  the ar market while the car would be a  major step into the ev and autonomy  industries either product could be a  great send-off for tim but if the final  product is the car then tim will likely  have to stick around for a lot longer  after all scaling ev production is no  easy task and given that tim has  publicly announced that he probably  won't be ceo in 10 years i'm inclined to  believe that his last product is likely  the air glasses launching an ev vehicle  would simply be a much longer commitment  not to mention the ar glasses are much  more aligned with apple's other products  the car is completely out of left field  and apple really has no prior experience  in even building a car also they've  already experienced several delays and  hurdles along the way and i wouldn't be  surprised if this project was scrapped  altogether  so the most likely scenario is that we  see apple glasses roll out in 2025 or  2026 followed by tim cook resigning near  the end of the decade in terms of a  successor the fan favorite seems to be  craig federighi which is not surprising  given his charisma and stage presence  but many experts feel that craig is  better suited to continue leading  software development as opposed to  becoming ceo  i gotta say though if craig did become  ceo apple's presentations would be quite  fun almost like steve jobs presentations  but we'll just have to wait and see in  the meantime apple stock has already  grown 10x under tim's leadership so at  this point for tim it's just a matter of  wrapping up his journey with a bow and  saying farewell when do you guys think  tim will retire and who do you think  will replace him comment that down below  also drop a like if you're excited for  tim's last product and of course  consider checking out our international  channels to watch our videos in other  languages and consider subscribing to  see more questions logically answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one

